[task.7.11]
title = "Fix: spec plugin load blocks on tracing every feature and times out"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/**", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_11.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_11.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 launch on 2026-10-24 (task 7.6, do_2) after the rebuild with 7.7-7.9: the running 2.2.0 server reports `plugin hyperfide-spec failed to load: load timed out after 30000ms`. Cause: platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/read-model-server.ts awaits SpecReader.start() for every repository before the plugin's default export resolves, and SpecReader.read() (src/reader.ts) runs `hyperfide spec status` and `hyperfide spec trace` sequentially for every feature directory under spec/ (18 features in this repository, 36 CLI runs; one `spec trace -f codify-v03 --json` ran over two minutes under load), so the load exceeds the server's loadTimeoutMs and the board, and the dispatch backend that reads features from this plugin, are gone. Fix: plugin load returns as soon as RPC is registered and the spec/ watcher is armed; the first read runs in the background and serves the cached snapshot (storage key hyperfide-spec:<repository>) or an empty snapshot flagged stale/refreshing until it lands, then publishes hyperfide-spec:changed; per-feature status/trace calls run with a bounded concurrency (4) instead of strictly sequentially; a feature whose trace fails or exceeds a per-feature budget is recorded as stale with its error instead of failing the whole read. No change to the RPC contract shape beyond the existing stale/error fields. The reader passes `--no-sync` to `spec trace` (graph sync belongs to the host daemon, not the board; measured on the owner's host: `spec trace -f codify-v04 --json` 50-86 s with sync, 17 s with --no-sync)."
do_2 = "7_11.sh boots the built server as 7_7.sh does with a project whose source is this repository and HYPERFIDE_BIN pointing at a wrapper script that delays every `spec trace` call by 3 seconds before delegating to the real binary; it asserts with `hyperfide-platform plugin list --json` that hyperfide-spec reaches running within 30 seconds with no error, that `plugin rpc call hyperfide-spec features` answers within 5 seconds (stale or empty allowed), and that within 240 seconds it returns all 18 features with hyperfide-v22 among them; the plugin's vitest suite covers the background first read, the bounded concurrency and the per-feature failure isolation."
reqs = ["3.1"]
owner = "spec"

[task.7.12]
title = "Fix: spec trace costs tens of seconds per feature"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["src/spec.c", "src/graph.c", "src/vcs.c", "src/memory.c", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_12.sh", "src/cg.h"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_12.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 relaunch on 2026-10-24 (task 7.6, do_2) while diagnosing 7.11: on the owner's host (16 cores, 7589 commits, .codegraph 2.7 GB, artifacts/hyperfide/tasks 91762 files) `hyperfide spec trace -f codify-v04 --json` (285-line feature) takes 50-86 s with the default graph sync and 17 s with --no-sync; `-f hyperfide-v22` 86 s; `-f codify-v03` over 10 minutes; `spec status` takes under 200 ms. The whole run is in-process CPU (strace shows no child processes). spec_trace_cmd (src/spec.c) opens the graph per call and trace_task resolves every task's symbols, touches (vcs_changed_paths over tagged commits) and memories (memory_query) one task at a time. Fix: profile trace on this repository, remove the per-task repeated work (one history scan per feature, one memory query per feature, symbol lookups by prepared statement or batch), make the default sync incremental or skip it when the graph is fresh, and bring `spec trace -f hyperfide-v22 --json --no-sync` under 2 s and with sync under 5 s on a warm graph without changing the JSON shape."
do_2 = "7_12.sh runs `hyperfide spec trace -f hyperfide-v22 --json --no-sync` and `--json` (default sync) on the repository with a warm graph, asserts both exit 0 with the same task set as before, and asserts the wall time is under 2 s and 5 s respectively (printing the timings); tests/hyperfide/cases for spec trace still pass."
reqs = ["3.1"]
owner = "source"

[task.7.13]
title = "Fix: the asks watcher re-lists and republishes on every graph write"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["platform/plugins/hyperfide-asks/src/bridge.ts", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-asks/src/bridge.test.ts", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_13.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_13.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24: the app sat on skeleton loaders for minutes behind a working HTTPS proxy. Cause: AskBridge.watch fs-watches .codegraph/graph.db and its WAL with a 40 ms debounce and no content check. The graph is the control plane and is written about ten times a second by the language server and indexers, so the server re-ran `hyperfide ask list --all --json` and broadcast the full list (39 KB) to every websocket client about 7.6 times a second (measured: 76 asks.changed signals, 3.0 MB in 10 s), sat at 50-70% CPU while idle, and pushed the boot endpoints system/config and provider-clis/status to 5-7 s."
do_2 = "bridge.ts keeps one watcher per repository shared by every subscriber: a listing runs at most once per minIntervalMs (1 s) after a debounceMs (250 ms) quiet time, writes arriving during a listing coalesce into one trailing listing, and asks.changed plus the subscribers' callbacks fire only when the serialized list differs from the one last published (answer() passes through the same gate). bridge.test.ts proves that a 20-write storm costs at most 3 listings and no publish, that a real change publishes exactly once, and that two subscribers share one watcher. 7_13.sh runs the plugin typecheck and suite and the bundled-plugins build, printing timings and exit codes only."
reqs = ["4.1"]
owner = "app"

[task.7.14]
title = "Fix: the spec read model broadcasts its whole snapshot on every spec edit"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/read-model-server.ts", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/reader.ts", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/shared/**", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/*.test.ts", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_14.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_14.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24 while fixing 7.13: registerSpecReadModel publishes the complete SpecSnapshot (features, status and trace of every feature; 1.38 MB serialized for this repository) on the realtime channel hyperfide-spec:changed after every .kvx write under spec/, to every websocket client, although no client code subscribes to that channel; clients refresh through api.rpc.experimental_invalidateAll, which the same callback already calls. Every fleet landing therefore pushes 1.4 MB to each open tab for nothing and stalls a slow link for seconds."
do_2 = "The change notice carries only what a subscriber needs to decide to refetch: repository, readAt, stale and error, and the ids of the features whose status or trace differ from the previous snapshot (the reader keeps the previous snapshot to diff); the full snapshot stays available over the spec RPCs and experimental_invalidateAll keeps firing. A unit test asserts the notice shape and that its serialized size stays under 4 KB for a snapshot with 60 tasks carrying trace evidence. 7_14.sh runs the hyperfide-spec typecheck and suite and the bundled-plugins build, printing timings and exit codes only."
reqs = ["3.1"]
owner = "app"

[task.7.15]
title = "Fix: the per-file semantic route purge scans every edge and FTS row on each indexed file"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["src/sem_routes.c", "src/semantic.c", "src/graph.c", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_15.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_15.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24: the owner's IDE language server (`hyperfide lsp`, 2.2.0) ran one index pass for over an hour at 100% CPU holding .codegraph/index.lock; perf put half the samples under sem_routes_purge_file. On the owner's graph (32,089 files, 364,461 symbols, 597,041 semantic_edges, 629 semantic_route_facts) `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` shows every purge statement as a full SCAN: the semantic_edges delete scans all edges because idx_sem_edge_source/target lead with branch_id, which the statement never filters; the semantic_route_facts subqueries scan because registration_file_id and handler_file_id are unindexed; the symbol_fts delete scans the virtual table because FTS5 cannot use `rowid IN (subquery)`. The purge runs once per indexed file, so a pass costs files x (edges + fts rows) even though almost no file registers a route."
do_2 = "sem_routes_purge_file first asks whether the file has any route facts (registration_file_id or handler_file_id, both indexed) and returns before touching other tables when it has none; the edge and composition deletes are rewritten to use existing or new indexes (branch-qualified or direct indexes on source_id/target_id, route_symbol_id, mount_route_id, child_route_id, registration_file_id), and the FTS rows are deleted by iterating the matched symbol ids and deleting each by rowid; ensure_route_schema adds the indexes to existing graphs. 7_15.sh builds the binary, creates a fixture graph (cg init on tests/fixtures/specrepo, then inserts 2,000 files, 200,000 semantic_edges and 50 route facts), asserts with `sqlite3 ... explain query plan` that no purge statement contains `SCAN semantic_edges`, `SCAN semantic_route_facts` or `SCAN symbol_fts`, and times a full `cg index` of that fixture under a limit of 10 s; it prints timings and exit codes only. tests/integration suites covering routes keep passing."
reqs = ["2.1"]
owner = "source"

[task.7.16]
title = "Fix: the qualification plugin's frontend bundle is emitted in a layout the server never loads"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.7"]
touches = ["platform/plugins/hyperfide-qualify/prepare.mjs", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-qualify/package.json", "platform/plugins/hyperfide-qualify/bundle.test.ts", "platform/packages/bundled-plugins/**", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_16.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_16.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24: the owner's browser received 404 (`bundle file missing`) for the Qualification plugin's app.js and app.css on every page load, five client retries each, and the Qualification panel never rendered. The server's app-bundle loader (platform/apps/server/src/services/plugins/app-bundle.ts, loadPluginAppBundle) reads `<pluginRoot>/dist/app.js`, `<pluginRoot>/dist/app.css` and `<pluginRoot>/dist/app.meta.json` and ignores the manifest's `hyperfide.app` path. hyperfide-qualify's own prepare.mjs (introduced with 7.7) bundles app.ts and server.ts into `.bundled-runtime/app.js` and `.bundled-runtime/server.js` at the package root with `hyperfide.app = ./app.js`, and writes no app.meta.json, so the plugin has no loadable frontend in the current layout; the hash the browser asked for came from a stale nested dist/app.js that the next bundled-plugins build removed."
do_2 = "Make the prepared hyperfide-qualify artifact use the layout every other bundled plugin uses and the loader reads: dist/server.js, dist/app.js (plus dist/app.css when styles exist), dist/app.meta.json and dist/server.meta.json with artifactFormatVersion 1, sdkMajor and sdkVersion of the running plugin SDK, and pluginId and pluginVersion matching package.json; the prepared manifest's hyperfide.server and hyperfide.app point at ./dist/server.js and ./dist/app.js. Prefer the shared prepare-bundled CLI (platform/packages/plugin-build) that hyperfide-spec uses; if a custom prepare.mjs stays it must emit the same files and keep 7.7's guarantee that the server bundle contains no `@hyperfide/plugin-sdk/` subpath import. bundle.test.ts asserts the prepared layout and the meta fields."
do_3 = "7_16.sh runs the plugin's prepare:bundled, asserts dist/app.js and dist/app.meta.json exist with pluginId hyperfide-qualify, builds @hyperfide/bundled-plugins..., then boots platform/apps/server/dist/index.js the way 7_7.sh does and asserts that /api/v1/plugins lists hyperfide-qualify with a non-null app.bundle and that GET on its advertised jsUrl returns 200 with a JavaScript body; it prints each step's seconds and exit code and ends with `ok: 7.16`."
reqs = ["5.1"]
owner = "qualification"

[task.7.17]
title = "Fix: the system version endpoint blocks boot on a public npm lookup for a package that is not ours"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.4"]
touches = ["platform/apps/server/src/services/system/app-version.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/services/system/app-version.test.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/services/system/app-update.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/routes/system.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/types.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/start-server.ts", "platform/packages/config/src/server.ts", "platform/packages/server-contract/src/api/system.ts", "platform/apps/app/src/components/settings/UpdatesSettingsSection.tsx", "platform/apps/app/src/components/settings/UpdatesSettingsSection.stories.tsx", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_17.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_17.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24: GET /api/v1/system/version awaits getSystemVersion, which fetches https://registry.npmjs.org/hyperfide-app/latest with a 5 s timeout (platform/apps/server/src/services/system/app-version.ts). From the owner's host the registry answers 404 after about 5 s, failures are never cached (only a non-null result is), so every boot of the UI blocks on this call; the owner's browser aborted it twice (HTTP 499 at the proxy). The response also advertises source `npm` and upgradeCommand `npx hyperfide-app@latest`, a package that is not ours (Hyperfide ships under its own identity, req.1)."
do_2 = "The endpoint never awaits the network: it answers at once with the current version and the last cached lookup, or latestVersion null and updateAvailable false, and refreshes in the background. Negative results (non-2xx, timeout, parse failure) are cached for a short TTL (10 min) so a failing registry costs one request per TTL, not one per boot. The remote check runs only when an update channel is configured through the server config; by default there is no network call, latestVersion is null, upgradeCommand is null and source reflects the install kind. The Updates settings section renders the no-channel state without an npm upgrade hint. The decision Ask `7.6: where should the platform check for updates?` was raised at filing with the recommendation off-until-channel; proceed on it and flag it in the commit."
do_3 = "app-version.test.ts covers: default config makes no fetch and returns latestVersion null; with a channel configured and a fetch that never resolves the service answers within 100 ms from cache or null; a 404 or throwing fetch is cached for the negative TTL and a second call inside the TTL makes no fetch. 7_17.sh runs the server typecheck and the app-version unit tests, then boots platform/apps/server/dist/index.js the way 7_7.sh does with no update channel configured and asserts GET /api/v1/system/version returns 200 in under 1 s with latestVersion null; it prints each step's seconds and exit code and ends with `ok: 7.17`."
do_4 = "Permission granted on 2026-10-24 by the feature manager: the touches now include the server runtime and config wiring and the system response contract (types.ts, start-server.ts, packages/config server.ts, server-contract api/system.ts) that a nullable upgradeCommand and a configurable update channel need. The owner answered Ask #19 off-until-channel and noted the package will later be published under the npm scope @sidioralabs/hyperfide, so the configurable channel accepts a scoped npm package name. A rerun travels with the branch of the previous attempt (refs/remotes/in1/task/v22-7.17): merge it and continue rather than starting over."
do_5 = "Baseline repair recorded on 2026-10-24 by the feature manager: the pre-existing npm-era suite platform/apps/server/test/system/app-version.test.ts asserted registry dist-tag lookups the rewritten service no longer performs and failed 15 assertions on the lane; it was removed because src/services/system/app-version.test.ts covers the explicit update channel, the no-request default, the bounded answer, failure caching and the transport timeout."
reqs = ["8.5"]
owner = "app"

[task.7.18]
title = "Fix: the index pass's resolve phase holds the graph write lock in one transaction for the whole pass"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.15"]
touches = ["src/scan.c", "src/semantic.c", "src/sem_routes.c", "src/progress.c", "src/index_chunks.h", "src/resolve.c", "src/sem_channels.c", "src/sem_dispatch.c", "src/sem_imports.c", "src/sem_incremental.c", "src/sem_resolve.c", "src/sem_types.c", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_18.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_18.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24: while the owner's IDE language server (`hyperfide lsp` 2.2.0) ran its index pass over the 2.7 GB graph, `hyperfide ask raise` failed four times in a row with `could not record the Ask: database is locked` (default CG_BUSY_TIMEOUT_MS 30000) and a probe `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` with a 20 s timeout failed as well: the write lock was held continuously for more than two minutes. The write phase in src/scan.c chunks its commits exactly as its design note says (the lock is the scarce resource; waiting writers land in the gaps), but the resolve phase (progress_begin_write at src/scan.c:1983, then anchor_edges, resolve_imports, resolve_refs and sem_incremental_run, COMMIT at src/scan.c:2010) is one transaction for the whole tree, so every other writer (asks, spec status, leases) fails instead of landing in a gap."
do_2 = "Bound the hold: the resolve and semantic phases commit in chunks the way the write phase does (per bounded unit of work, or after at most about two seconds of lock time), releasing and re-acquiring the write lock between chunks; chunks already committed stay valid when a later one is abandoned, and the pass bookkeeping (index_health_finish and the pkey meta) is written only when the last chunk commits, so a stalled run stays recoverable exactly as today. A full pass over a fixture produces the same rows, edges and route facts as before."
do_3 = "7_18.sh indexes a synthetic fixture large enough that the resolve plus semantic phase takes more than 20 s in the sandbox while a prober loop runs `BEGIN IMMEDIATE; ROLLBACK;` against the graph with a 5 s busy timeout once a second; it asserts that no probe fails during the pass and that the longest observed lock wait is under 5 s, and that the resulting graph matches an unchunked reference pass on the counts of files, symbols, semantic_edges and semantic_route_facts; it prints each step's seconds and exit code and ends with `ok: 7.18`."
do_4 = "Decision recorded on 2026-10-24 by the feature manager (the worker's decision Ask, proceed-on-recommendation): the resolve and semantic write passes materialize driving cursors into disk-backed work lists and commit at cooperative checkpoints, which touches the resolver and every semantic loop file; those files are now in touches. The alternative, keyset pagination in every pass, was not taken."
reqs = ["2.1"]
owner = "source"

[task.7.19]
title = "Fix: the Fleet and Image builder pages are empty and unstyled because the platform never starts its dispatcher and the image builder ignores the registered provider"
status = "done"
wave = 7
requires = ["7.9"]
touches = ["platform/plugins/hyperfide-dispatch/**", "platform/packages/plugin-sdk/src/machine-provider.ts", "platform/packages/plugin-sdk/src/backend-contract.ts", "platform/packages/plugin-sdk/src/host-contract.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/services/plugins/plugin-api.ts", "platform/apps/server/src/services/hosts/**", "platform/plugins/machine-railway/src/provider.ts", "platform/plugins/machine-railway/src/machine-ops.ts", "platform/plugins/machine-blaxel/src/provider.ts", "platform/packages/plugin-sdk/src/internal/host-policy.ts", "platform/packages/plugin-sdk/src/__tests__/public-types.test.ts", "tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_19.sh"]
verify_cmd = "bash tests/hyperfide/cases/v22/7_19.sh"
do_1 = "Defect found at the 7.6 owner test on 2026-10-24. /plugins/hyperfide-dispatch/fleet renders `No machines`, `No jobs`, `No task events` and `Cap: 0 / 0`: 14 fleetLedger calls answered 200 with an empty ledger. Cause: the plugin's default export (platform/plugins/hyperfide-dispatch/server.ts) registers the fleet RPC over createServerBackend, which reads job ledgers and `fleet.control` from the plugin data directory, but nothing in the platform constructs or ticks Dispatcher (src/dispatcher/dispatcher.ts): `new Dispatcher(` appears only in tests. The read model also leaves `started` and `finished` null and `timelines` empty, so req.6 ac_6 is unmet. /plugins/hyperfide-dispatch/image-builder polls imageBuilds every second (66 calls in one owner session), accepts only a 40-hex commit, and imageBuildStart throws unless the hidden kv keys dispatch.repository and dispatch.fleetPath are set, then always throws `Registered machine providers do not support image checkpoints` because registerServerImageBuilder (src/server-backend.ts) hardcodes a stub machines object with checkpoint false instead of using the registered provider, although the Railway provider checkpoints (platform/plugins/machine-railway/src/provider.ts). Both views are raw HTML (bare main, button, table, pre) while sibling plugins build on the shared UI kit through tsconfig paths `@/*` to packages/shared-ui/src/* (see platform/plugins/hyperfide-asks/views/inbox-view.tsx and platform/plugins/hyperfide-spec/src/board/views/board-panel.tsx: ResourceCollectionPage, ResourceToolbar, ResourceListPanel, DelayedLoading, Input, Label, Icon); the dispatch tsconfig has no such paths."
do_2 = "The plugin starts one Dispatcher per server in its default export: data directory under experimental_dataDir/plugins/<pluginId>, spec source bound through the hyperfide-spec RPC read model, machine providers from api.sdk.hosts.experimental_listProviders(), limits from the concurrency-limit snapshot, gateway keys from plugin settings; it ticks on an interval (15 s default) and after backoff, and stops on dispose. Safety default: `fleet.control` starts paused with cap 0, so nothing is claimed until an operator resumes and raises the cap from the Fleet page; with no machine provider configured the ledger reports that state in words. The ledger records started and finished timestamps per job and per-task timeline events (claimed, started, finished or failed, merged) with log tails taken from the dispatcher's own writes. A new RPC fleetImportLedgers accepts JobLedger records with optional started, finished, worker and log fields plus timeline events and upserts them into the plugin storage, so jobs run by other tooling become visible; imported jobs carry their source."
do_3 = "The image builder uses the registered machine providers' create and checkpoint operations instead of the stub; dispatch.repository and dispatch.fleetPath become declared plugin settings (settings.define) shown with a not-configured state inline on the page; the commit field accepts a branch, tag or short sha and resolves it through the repository; polling is 5 s or realtime."
do_4 = "Both views are rebuilt on the shared UI kit with the same tsconfig paths as hyperfide-asks: a collection page with toolbar, filters and search over machines and jobs, a detail pane with the log tail, status chips, per-task timelines, and the budget controls as a settings-style section; loading, empty and error states use DelayedLoading and alerts like the sibling plugins. No credential or key value is ever rendered; keys remain a count."
do_5 = "Tests: server-backend.test.ts covers the dispatcher starting paused with cap 0 and no providers (ledger reports paused, idle, no providers), fleetImportLedgers upserting records that fleetLedger then returns with timelines, and the image builder calling the registered provider; view tests render the populated and empty ledgers. 7_19.sh runs the plugin typecheck and tests and the bundled-plugins build, boots the built server the way 7_7.sh does, asserts fleetLedger returns budget.paused true, imports a fixture ledger through fleetImportLedgers and asserts fleetLedger lists it with a timeline; it prints each step's seconds and exit code and ends with `ok: 7.19`."
do_6 = "Permission granted on 2026-10-24 by the feature manager: the registered machine-provider contract may be widened, additively and capability-gated, with checkpoint creation, checkpoint-based allocation and command execution, across the plugin SDK contract files, the server plugin API and hosts services, and the Railway and Blaxel provider bridges now listed in touches. A rerun travels with the branch of the previous attempt (refs/remotes/in1/task/v22-7.19): merge it and continue rather than starting over."
do_7 = "Recorded on 2026-10-24 by the feature manager: the rerun also had to register the new host capability in the SDK host policy and its public-types test, two consequential two-line edits now listed in touches."
reqs = ["6.3", "6.4", "6.6"]
owner = "platform"

